gb-022-cause-2 = the enrollment Go module is not at ops/enrollment
gb-023-title = rollback refused or guided by artifact type
gb-023-what = the component's artifact type is data, cert, forward-only or reversible:false (refused, exit 3), or config/acl (guided, exit 4): its rollback is not a pin flip.
gb-023-cause-1 = the component carries a migration or a certificate
gb-024-title = the fixture is not a shape the mock converger knows
gb-024-what = on --mock, converger.yaml names a behaviour the scripted converger does not know, a record is unreadable, or nothing is declared for the environment.
gb-024-cause-1 = a hand-edited fixture
gb-025-title = the config does not validate
gb-025-what = ops/goatbuilder/goatbuilder.yaml failed the versioned schema or a cross-field rule. The config is generated; fix the source and regenerate.
gb-025-cause-1 = a hand edit to the generated file
gb-025-cause-2 = a pins or ladder change the generator was not run for
gb-026-title = status reads real hosts only through a converger
gb-026-what = without --mock, status has no converger to read hosts through yet.
gb-026-cause-1 = the push converger's status path is not built
gb-027-title = not inside a repository
gb-027-what = the repository root could not be resolved from the working directory and -C was not given.
gb-027-cause-1 = run outside a checkout
gb-028-title = a command the verb runs failed
gb-028-what = git, gh, crane, python or another tool exited non-zero; its own words follow and are interpreted below when the catalog knows them.
gb-028-cause-1 = see the tool's message
gb-029-title = the play failed on a group
gb-029-what = ansible-playbook exited non-zero for the group; the log path is in the row.
gb-029-cause-1 = a fatal task on a host (see the ansible entries below)
gb-029-cause-2 = a host unreachable over the chosen transport
gb-030-title = no host of the group is in the loaded inventories
gb-030-what = the group is declared in the pins but the inventories loaded for this goatnet carry no host for it, so nothing was deployed (declared, not built).
gb-030-cause-1 = a site declared ahead of its hardware
gb-030-cause-2 = the wrong inventory pair for this goatnet
gb-031-title = an op:// reference in your shell is incomplete
gb-031-what = `op run` validates every environment variable whose value starts with op://, not only the env-file, and refuses the whole run when one has fewer than vault/item/field.
gb-031-cause-1 = a variable exported from your shell profile for another project holds a two-part op:// reference
gb-032-title = the candidate record is open but the goatnet runs a different ref
gb-032-what = graduate.py's C6 check: a record for this component is still open while the goatnet now runs another ref — the record the preprod step superseded was never abandoned.
gb-032-cause-1 = a release run on a checkout older than #2039, which abandons the superseded record in the preprod change
gb-032-cause-2 = a candidate re-cut while the previous record stayed open
gb-033-title = promote refused a graduation step
gb-033-what = `goatbuilder promote` (the release ladder, ADR 1120/1125) refused: the record, the set, the ladder or the ref does not meet the rule the step names. Nothing graduates by itself; a refusal changes nothing.
gb-033-cause-1 = the component is not ready (soak, live rows, convergence evidence) — the lines under the refusal name each blocker
gb-033-cause-2 = the component is a member of an open graduation set and must graduate with it
gb-033-cause-3 = a ref that is not digest-pinned (…@sha256:…), or a hotfix without --ack
gb-033-cause-4 = no open record for the component, or the record named does not exist or already graduated
gb-034-title = the generated pins or intents differ from fleet-pins.yaml
gb-034-what = `goatbuilder pins --check` regenerated every member's pins.yml and every goatnet's intent from ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml and they differ from what is committed, or an intent is unsigned or tampered, or a governed pin is defined outside pins.yml.
gb-034-cause-1 = fleet-pins.yaml changed without regenerating
gb-034-cause-2 = a hand edit to a generated pins.yml or intent
gb-035-title = the goatnet's intent names no usable CA
gb-035-what = an intent has no ca_id, or one from which the CA's directory under <deploy.state_dir> cannot be derived, so it cannot be signed.
gb-035-cause-1 = a new goatnet whose intent was written without ca_id
gb-036-title = the component declares no image workflow
gb-036-what = release finds a candidate's image build by the component's image_workflow in fleet-pins.yaml, and there is none.
gb-036-cause-1 = a component added without image_workflow
gb-037-title = the ladder is missing a live rung
gb-037-what = ops/release/ladder.yaml has no live rung with a goatnet where the step needs one, or does not read.
gb-037-cause-1 = a rung marked not live while a goatnet is rebuilt
gb-037-cause-2 = a malformed ladder.yaml
gb-038-title = --from preprod-rows cannot resume this candidate
gb-038-what = resuming at the preprod rows needs preprod to run the candidate on main and exactly one open record for it; one of the two is missing.
gb-038-cause-1 = the preprod pin was never landed for this candidate
gb-038-cause-2 = the record was closed, or a second one opened
gb-039-title = a rollback on a fixture needs its environment
gb-039-what = on a fixture (--mock) a rollback declares the earlier ref in one environment and converges it there, so it needs --env; --list and --status read host ledgers, which a fixture does not have.
gb-039-cause-1 = --mock given without --env
gb-039-cause-2 = --list or --status given with --mock
gb-040-title = that directory is not a tutor workspace
gb-040-what = the tutor creates its workspace in an empty or new directory and deletes only a directory that carries its .goatbuilder-tutor marker; it refused to use or delete one that does not.
gb-040-cause-1 = --workspace (or $GOATBUILDER_TUTOR) points at a directory with other files in it
gb-041-title = there is no such lesson
gb-041-what = the tutor was asked for a lesson number it does not have.
gb-041-cause-1 = a number past the last lesson
gb-042-title = the live rows cannot run from this machine — the candidate is UNTESTED, not dead
gb-042-what = a release step reached the live acceptance rows, and this machine lacks what they need (the console's Playwright toolchain for a browser row, or a tool or context the runner reported with exit 2). The rows did not run; nothing about the candidate failed.
gb-042-cause-1 = a browser row is mapped and ops/goat-id-console/web/e2e/node_modules is missing, or node/npm is not installed
gb-042-cause-2 = the acceptance runner exited 2 (a tool or a goat context missing on this machine or on the host it runs goat on)
gb-043-title = the door to the rung did not open for its live rows
gb-043-what = the rows for a rung this laptop is not meshed to run goat on that rung's management host through its door (goat_via door:<env>), and the door did not open or printed no ssh target.
gb-043-cause-1 = aws-vault cannot reach the declared AWS profile (signed out, a malformed credential, or an expiring session)
gb-043-cause-2 = session-manager-plugin is not installed
gb-044-title = a release refuses before the tag because a secret is not set
gb-044-what = every rung this run will deploy to templates the component's secrets from your environment, and at least one is not set. Nothing was tagged, pinned or deployed.
gb-044-cause-1 = the run was not wrapped in op run with a .refs file naming those variables
gb-044-cause-2 = goat-credentials refs/goat.refs has no line for a variable (ops/ci/check-secrets-coverage.py names it)
gb-045-title = the published declarations are out of order or altered
gb-045-what = a declaration is applied only from an intact, ordered log (contract C2). A row whose signature does not match its content, or a serial not greater than one already accepted, is refused before anything is published, so a replayed or edited declaration never reaches a machine. A new serial at an earlier digest is accepted; that is a revert.
gb-045-cause-1 = a row of declared/publications.jsonl was edited by hand after it was published
gb-045-cause-2 = an older copy of the log was restored over a newer one, or a row was replayed
gb-046-title = the local declaration is not the source of record's
gb-046-what = a deploy applies only what the source of record declares (contract C3). A local copy behind it is fast-forwarded first, or refused under --frozen; a local copy ahead of it or diverged from it is always refused, because a declaration that is not in the source of record is never applied.
gb-046-cause-1 = the checkout or the fixture's declared/ was not fast-forwarded and --frozen was passed
gb-046-cause-2 = a declaration was edited locally instead of in the source of record
gb-047-title = a declaration must name who made it
gb-047-what = every declaration, and every record it converges, carries the person who made it (contract C4). A deploy, rollback or standup run without --ack is refused before anything is published.
gb-047-cause-1 = --ack was not passed, or was empty
gb-048-title = the goatnet is not proven built
gb-048-what = the acceptance assay ran and at least one applicable row failed, or could not answer, or no row passed. Only PASS counts toward done; an UNKNOWN row is a row nobody proved (ADR 1115).
gb-048-cause-1 = a FAIL row is the goatnet failing that check; its caught_would_have text says what it means
gb-048-cause-2 = an UNKNOWN row could not be probed from here, most often a goat CLI row run where no goat context with a fabric identity exists (E_PROFILE_MISSING)
gb-048-cause-3 = a SKIPPED negative row whose paired positive did not pass
gb-049-title = the acceptance assay cannot run here
gb-049-what = assay derives the site's values from its group_vars and runs ops/validation/run-acceptance.py with python3; one of those could not start, so nothing was probed.
gb-049-cause-1 = the site name has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/vars.yml, or --values names no file
gb-049-cause-2 = python3 or PyYAML is not installed
gb-049-cause-3 = the runner stopped with an error rather than a verdict
gb-050-title = nothing is declared on that goatnet
gb-050-what = diff or adopt found no fleet-pins component whose member groups include the goatnet.
gb-050-cause-1 = the goatnet key is right but no component is pinned there yet
gb-050-cause-2 = every component is absent on it
gb-051-title = the plan request is incomplete or not planned yet
gb-051-what = plan writes deploy and rollback plans; a deploy plan needs --env, and a rollback plan reads a host ledger, so it has no fixture form.
gb-051-cause-1 = plan deploy without --env
gb-051-cause-2 = a verb other than deploy or rollback
gb-051-cause-3 = plan rollback with --mock
gb-052-title = that file is not a goatbuilder plan
gb-052-what = apply runs only a file plan wrote, which carries schema goatbuilder.plan/v1.
gb-052-cause-1 = the wrong file was passed to apply
gb-052-cause-2 = the plan was edited by hand and its schema line lost
gb-053-title = the plan no longer matches the tree
gb-053-what = apply recomputed the plan's decisions and at least one moved since it was written; it names each, and runs nothing.
gb-053-cause-1 = main's declaration or the pins file changed since the plan
gb-053-cause-2 = a newer deploy or rollback moved the rollback target
gb-053-cause-3 = the plan is old
gb-054-title = adopt could not create its worktree
gb-054-what = adopt --yes writes its declarations into a fresh worktree of origin/main beside the checkout; git refused to create it.
gb-054-cause-1 = origin/main is not fetched
gb-054-cause-2 = a branch or directory of that name already exists
gb-055-title = a rollback with no --site would flip every goatnet
gb-055-what = the component is deployed on more than one goatnet and no --site was given; with no site the roll narrows to nothing, so it would flip every goatnet at once, prod included. Nothing was touched; the error lists the goatnets to choose from.
gb-055-cause-1 = rollback (or plan rollback) was run on a component that spans several goatnets without --site
gb-055-cause-2 = the goatnet to roll back was left off the command
gb-060-title = a standup input is required and not set
gb-060-what = A role or play the site's standup will reach asserts this variable, or reads it through `| mandatory`, and nothing the site supplies sets it: not group_vars, the inventory, host_vars, the play's vars, a role default, nor an extra var the plan passes. The scan lists every such input at once, before any host is touched.
gb-060-cause-1 = the site's config surface was made from a reference goatnet and this key was not carried over
gb-060-cause-2 = the role gained a required input after the site was configured
gb-061-title = a secret the standup templates is not in this environment
gb-061-what = A value the site supplies is lookup('env', NAME), a play or role the standup reaches uses it, and the declared secrets posture cannot supply NAME (ops/secrets/posture.py, ADR 1204).
gb-061-cause-1 = the run is not under the site's secrets posture
gb-061-cause-2 = the posture's references file has no line for this name
gb-062-title = a standup input may be required
gb-062-what = The input is required under a `when:` condition the scan cannot evaluate from the site's own values (it depends on a registered result, a fact, or a template). This is a warning, never a refusal: the scan does not claim more than it knows.
gb-062-cause-1 = the condition reads a value only known when the play runs
gb-063-title = a standup step's host limit selects no host
gb-063-what = The plan runs this play with --limit <pattern>, and the pattern matches no host of the target group in the inventory, so the play runs on nothing and reports success.
gb-063-cause-1 = the limit assumes the mgmt-<site>-01 host naming and this site names its hosts differently
gb-063-cause-2 = the step is meant for the founder only and this site is a joiner
gb-064-title = the checkout is behind the trunk in what a deploy reads
gb-064-what = A real run reads ops/ansible (roles, plays, group_vars, inventory, the rendered pins) and ops/pins from the checkout it runs in. The trunk carries commits to those paths that this checkout lacks, so a file rendered from it would remove on the host what those commits added, and the play would report success (contract C3, F-410). Refused before any host is touched, in both avenues (ops/scripts/fresh-source.sh).
gb-064-cause-1 = the checkout was not brought onto the trunk after a merge
gb-064-cause-2 = a feature branch was cut before a change to a role, group_vars or the pins landed
gb-065-title = the trunk could not be fetched, so the checkout cannot be shown current
gb-065-what = A real run proves its checkout is current with the trunk before any host is touched (contract C3). The trunk remote did not answer a fetch, so that proof is missing and the run is refused. An islanded site, cut off by design, runs with --offline: it is judged against the cached trunk and the ledger row it writes says source: offline.
gb-065-cause-1 = no route to the git remote (the laptop is offline, the forge is down)
gb-065-cause-2 = the remote's credentials are not loaded (an ssh key not in the agent)
gb-066-title = the site's declared end state cannot be derived or read
gb-066-what = endstate derives what a site should run from its own declarations (group_vars, the fleet pin table, the inventory) and the service catalog, then reads the site's hosts once over Ansible. One of those could not start, so nothing was graded.
gb-066-cause-1 = the site name has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/ directory
gb-066-cause-2 = python3 or PyYAML is not installed on this machine
gb-066-cause-3 = the catalog file ops/goatbuilder/endstate/catalog.yaml does not parse
gb-067-title = a site's service presence is undeclared or contradicted
gb-067-what = Every service the catalog lists must be declared present or absent for every governed site, by exactly one answer: a flag in the site's group_vars, the fleet pin table (an absent deviation), or the founder rule. The baseline ops/goatbuilder/endstate/baseline.txt lists the gaps that exist today and may only shrink: a new gap, or a baseline row that is no longer true, fails.
gb-067-cause-1 = a new service in the catalog with no flag declared for a site
gb-067-cause-2 = the fleet pin table renders a component the site's flag says it does not run (or the reverse)
gb-067-cause-3 = a gap was closed and its baseline row was left behind
gb-068-title = the goatnet is not in its declared end state
gb-068-what = endstate graded every service the site declares: present or absent, deployed (its ledger names the declared ref), active (every container running), healthy (its healthcheck or isitup probe), functional (the acceptance rows that exercise it), and its zero-trust edges (its served name resolves and its certificate verifies, its OIDC client is registered in Hydra, its SPIRE entry exists). At least one grade failed or could not be read, or something runs that the end state does not declare (an orphan).
gb-068-cause-1 = a declared service is not running, or its ledger is pending, rolled back or at another ref
gb-068-cause-2 = an edge is broken, such as an OIDC client never registered or a name that does not resolve
gb-068-cause-3 = an orphan, such as a container left behind by a retired service, or a Hydra client or SPIRE entry no service declares
gb-068-cause-4 = a grade could not be read, most often because a host was unreachable or the assay did not run
gb-069-title = the rollout preflight cannot run
gb-069-what = preflight reads the component's declared rollout facts from ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml (owns_state, rollback, restore_from, coupled_with) and the open graduation sets. The component is not in the pins, or the pins could not be read, so there is nothing to grade.
gb-069-cause-1 = the component name is misspelled or not a fleet-pins component key
gb-069-cause-2 = ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml is missing, unreadable, or did not parse
gb-069-cause-3 = on a fixture (--mock), declared/artifacts.yaml did not parse
gb-070-title = survey could read no goatnet at all
gb-070-what = survey reads every goatnet it was asked for in parallel and reports what it could not read as unknown. When not one goatnet answered, there is nothing to compare, and the run stops instead of printing an empty survey that would look consistent.
gb-070-cause-1 = this laptop is on no goatnet's mesh profile and no door opened (no AWS credentials for the SSM door)
gb-070-cause-2 = every goatnet named with --env is misspelled or not in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml
gb-070-cause-3 = ansible is not installed, or ops/scripts/with-aws cannot run
gb-071-title = a site's leaf certificate is not on this controller
gb-071-what = A play that installs a site's leaf (the zenoh router, the LAN mgmt identity, the dashboard pair, the names collector) found the cert or key nowhere it looks: the site manifest's out_dir (<deploy.state_dir>/<site>-leaves), then a copy staged in this checkout or the main checkout. It refuses before touching a host.
gb-071-cause-1 = the leaf was never minted for this site, or was minted on another operator's machine
gb-071-cause-2 = the manifest's out_dir points somewhere this machine does not have
gb-071-cause-3 = GOAT_LEAF_DIR is set to a directory that does not hold it
gb-072-title = goatbuilder cannot name the download surface to update from
gb-072-what = update (and the behind-version notice) reads the release a goatnet serves at get.<site_id>.<site_dns_zone>/goatbuilder/. It looks at GOATBUILDER_UPDATE_URL, then --site (named from the site's group_vars), then the surface the last successful update used. None of them named a surface that serves goatbuilder for this machine.
gb-072-cause-1 = the first update on this machine was run without --site, and GOATBUILDER_UPDATE_URL is not set
gb-072-cause-2 = the site named with --site has no ops/ansible/group_vars/<site>_hosts/ in the checkout goatbuilder was run in
gb-072-cause-3 = the site does not serve goatbuilder (goat_get_goatbuilder_enabled is not true in its group_vars)
gb-072-cause-4 = no goatbuilder release is built for this operating system and architecture
gb-073-title = the goatbuilder download surface could not be read
gb-073-what = update reads version.json, the platform binary and its .sha256 from get.<site>.<zone>/goatbuilder/. One of them did not arrive: the connection failed, the server answered with an error, the answer was not what the surface publishes, or the binary stopped arriving for 30 seconds. Nothing was installed; the binary in place is the one that ran.
gb-073-cause-1 = this machine is not on the goatnet's mesh (get.<site> is mesh-only)
gb-073-cause-2 = the surface is mid-deploy, or does not publish this platform's binary
gb-073-cause-3 = the link stalled; a slow link is fine, a silent one for 30 seconds is not
gb-074-title = the downloaded goatbuilder is not vouched for by its release
gb-074-what = Before touching anything, update checks that the <asset>.sha256 the surface serves agrees with the release's SHA256SUMS, that the SHA256SUMS signature verifies against the release workflow's identity (when cosign is on PATH), and that the downloaded binary hashes to that digest. One of these failed, so it refused to install; the installed binary is unchanged.
gb-074-cause-1 = the surface is mid-deploy and the binary, its checksum and SHA256SUMS are from different releases
gb-074-cause-2 = the download was altered or truncated on the way
gb-074-cause-3 = the signature over SHA256SUMS is not from the release workflow of the repository goatbuilder.yaml names
gb-075-title = goatbuilder could not replace its own binary
gb-075-what = The download was verified, but writing it next to the running binary or renaming it into place failed. The rename is atomic, so the binary in place is either the old one, whole, or the new one, whole.
gb-075-cause-1 = the install directory is not writable by this user (a binary under /usr/local/bin installed with sudo)
gb-075-cause-2 = the disk is full
gb-076-title = the rollout is not cleanly reversible or not ready
gb-076-what = preflight graded the three rollout facts (State, Rollback, Scope) and the readiness half, and at least one row is not a clean, declared, validated pass. An undeclared fact fails closed (stateful, rollback none) and reads not-clean on purpose, so the gap is visible rather than guessed green; an UNKNOWN is not a pass either. The facts are still printed — a stateful, restore-required rollout is a valid state to deploy knowingly, with --ack.
gb-076-cause-1 = a fact is undeclared, so it failed closed (declare owns_state / rollback / restore_from / coupled_with in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml)
gb-076-cause-2 = a declared rollback contradicts the artifact type (pin-revert over a data/cert/forward-only component, or one flagged reversible false)
gb-076-cause-3 = a readiness check failed — the ref is not pullable, pins --check found drift, the secrets gate is unsatisfied, the checkout is stale, or the end state is not declared
gb-076-cause-4 = a validation could not answer (UNKNOWN) — no registry probe, or no reachable ledger for the rollback --dry-run
gb-100-title = the federation verbs cannot find this party's context directory
gb-100-what = goatbuilder federate init (and verify or apply without --as) reads this party's declaration, federation.toml, and init also reads the mTLS certificate and CA roots beside it. goatbuilder has no profile of its own, so it takes the directory from --context-dir, else $GOAT_CONTEXT_DIR, else the goat profile ($GOAT_PROFILE_DIR, else ~/.goat), and none of those was set.
gb-100-cause-1 = none of --context-dir, GOAT_CONTEXT_DIR, GOAT_PROFILE_DIR or HOME is set, as in a bare container or a service unit
gb-110-title = the inventory does not name a federation host phase 3 can reach
gb-110-what = federate phase 3 measures the edge and the core from this machine, reaching each host the way the peering plays do: the host in the edge group and the host in the core group of inventory/peering-edges.ini, with its address, user, port, key and SSH options. The inventory could not be read, the group has no host or several, or the host uses a connection phase 3 does not use.
gb-110-cause-1 = ansible-inventory is not installed here, or the inventory does not parse
gb-110-cause-2 = the edge group (<partner>_edge) or the core group (<partner>_core) is empty or missing in inventory/peering-edges.ini
gb-110-cause-3 = the group has several hosts and none was named
gb-110-cause-4 = the host uses an ansible_connection other than ssh or local
gb-111-title = a federation host did not answer over SSH
gb-111-what = Before measuring anything, phase 3 runs `true` on the edge and on the core over SSH, with the inventory's connection settings and the plays' privilege escalation. That command failed, so nothing was measured. The message carries the ssh command it ran.
gb-111-cause-1 = this machine is not on a network that reaches the host (mesh profile, door or jump host)
gb-111-cause-2 = the host refused the key (see X-SSH-001)
gb-111-cause-3 = the host key changed (see X-SSH-003)
gb-112-title = sudo on a federation host wants a password
gb-112-what = The host answered over SSH, but `sudo -n true` failed: phase 3 reads the federation interface and the container runtime as root, the way the plays become root, and it never types a password.
gb-112-cause-1 = the host's operator user is not NOPASSWD in sudoers (the baseline play sets it)
gb-112-cause-2 = ansible_become_user names a user this account cannot become without a password
gb-113-title = no core router to measure on the core host
gb-113-what = Phase 3 measures the core router's image and version on the core host. It takes the router the operator names with --router-container, else the name the deployment's variables give (zenoh_stack, or site_id with the goat-zenoh role's default, as <zenoh_stack>-router), else the one running core router discovery finds. That router was not running, could not be read, or several candidates were found.
gb-113-cause-1 = the declared router is not running on the core host, or the variables give a different name than the one deployed
gb-113-cause-2 = nothing declares the router and no running container carries org.goat.component=zenoh-core or a standard name
gb-113-cause-3 = several core routers run on the host
gb-120-title = a peering names a partner with a reserved word
gb-120-what = A word of `goatbuilder federate` (init lint compose diff verify apply help status plan deploy test allocate) can never name a partner: `goatbuilder federate <word>` runs that step, so the peering could never be run by name.
gb-120-cause-1 = a peerings entry's `with:` is one of the reserved words
gb-121-title = two peerings would share a value
gb-121-what = A per-peering value (an interface, a UDP or TCP port, a link subnet, a forwarder port, a certificate directory, an inventory name) is pinned on one peering and already held by another, by a pin or by the allocation record, or a partner is declared twice on one environment. Two peerings on one edge or core host cannot share one.
gb-121-cause-1 = two peerings pin the same value under values:
gb-121-cause-2 = a pin takes a value the allocation record holds for another peering
gb-121-cause-3 = the same partner is declared twice on one environment
gb-122-title = a pinned port is one the edge host already holds
gb-122-what = A pinned WireGuard or carrier listen port is a UDP port the environment's edge hosts already hold (environments[].peering_edge_reserved_udp; absent, NetBird's WireGuard on 51820). A second listener there fails or steals its traffic.
gb-122-cause-1 = values.wg_listen_port or values.carrier_listen_port is pinned to a reserved port
gb-122-cause-2 = a peering running on the role default 51820, on edges with no mesh client, is being adopted
gb-123-title = a peering's pin is not usable
gb-123-what = A pin under a peering's values: is malformed (an interface name over 15 characters, a subnet that is not a /31 or /30 on its boundary, a relative certificate directory, a name with spaces), or pins a forwarder value on a peering no forwarder relays.
gb-123-cause-1 = a typing mistake in a pin
gb-123-cause-2 = public_port or a carrier_* value pinned without forwarder:
gb-124-title = the peering allocation record cannot be used
gb-124-what = ops/goatbuilder/peering-values.yaml, the record of the per-peering values goatbuilder federate allocate chose, is unreadable, has another schema, holds a malformed value, gives two peerings one value, or records a port the edge host already holds. Allocation reads it so a re-run never moves a value; it will not guess around a broken one.
gb-124-cause-1 = a hand edit or a merge conflict in the record
gb-124-cause-2 = a record from a newer goatbuilder
gb-125-title = a peering file exists that goatbuilder did not generate
gb-125-what = goatbuilder federate allocate writes the peering inventory and each peering's peering-values.yml. It never overwrites a file it did not generate (its first line says who did), and never renders a variable a hand-written file already sets for the same hosts (the group, its parent peering group, or the host_vars of its inventory names), because Ansible's file order would then pick the value. Nothing was written.
gb-125-cause-1 = ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini or a peering-values.yml was written by hand before the values were allocated
gb-125-cause-2 = a hand-written group_vars or host_vars file sets a variable allocate renders (a listen port, a certificate path)
gb-126-title = a peering's inventory group is defined twice
gb-126-what = Not every peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini stays hand-written and each peering that declares hosts: gets its own rendered inventory file (inventory/peering-<edge_group>.ini), which the driver reads beside it. That peering's group is also defined in the hand-written inventory, so the plays would see it twice. Nothing was written.
gb-126-cause-1 = hosts: added to a peering whose group is still in the hand-written peering-edges.ini
gb-127-title = no free per-peering value is left
gb-127-what = Every candidate in a per-peering value's sequence is held by another peering of the same environment (128 interfaces, ports or /31 subnets), or by a pin.
gb-127-cause-1 = many peerings on one environment, or pins scattered over the sequence
gb-128-title = a federate run disagrees with the peering's allocated values
gb-128-what = The allocation record holds this peering, and the run's working directory, edge group or core group is not the allocated one. Phase 8 would mint certificates where the edge does not read them, or the plays would run on another peering's hosts. Nothing ran.
gb-128-cause-1 = --work or FED_WORK names a directory whose pki is not the allocated cert_dir
gb-128-cause-2 = --edge-group names another group than the allocated edge_group
gb-128-cause-3 = a partner declared on a second environment, or a core_group pin: the driver runs the republisher on <with>_core and does not read the allocated core group yet
gb-129-title = a peering's offer would widen what federation.toml declares
gb-129-what = federation.toml owns the release sets this party offers ([data] release). A peering's offer: may only narrow that list for one partner. It names a set federation.toml does not offer, is empty or malformed, or federation.toml cannot be read. Or the peering's federation.toml is hand-written (federation_toml: hand), whose [data] release phase 3 offers whole, and offer: is narrower than it; a peering whose federation.toml goatbuilder federate allocate generates gets the narrowed list. Nothing ran.
gb-129-cause-1 = offer: names a set that is not in federation.toml's [data] release
gb-129-cause-2 = offer: is narrower than the [data] release of a hand-written federation.toml (federation_toml: hand), which phase 3 offers whole
gb-129-cause-3 = GOAT_PROFILE_DIR or GOAT_CONTEXT points at another goat context than the one phase 3 runs in
gb-210-title = the host firewall baseline is missing a value it needs
gb-210-what = The host firewall baseline (roles/goat-host-firewall, rendered by its goat-host-firewall-render.py) could not build a ruleset, because a value it derives the rules from is missing or malformed. Nothing was applied.
gb-210-cause-1 = goat_host_firewall_management_sources is empty, so SSH would be admitted from nowhere
gb-210-cause-2 = an edge run reached the role without its peering values (the play was not peering-wg.yml or peering-edge.yml)
gb-210-cause-3 = a port, address or interface name in the input is not one (a partner endpoint given as a name, an interface name over 15 characters)
gb-210-cause-4 = a management source is /0, the mesh interface is the host's uplink, or a br-* bridge that is not docker's (a VXLAN segment bridge) exists while container-bridges is kept
gb-210-cause-5 = twice goat_host_firewall_confirm_timeout plus 15 s is not below goat_host_firewall_rollback_seconds
gb-210-cause-6 = the switch is false but the host still carries the baseline (/etc/goat-host-firewall/profile exists)
gb-211-title = the host firewall override names a rule the baseline does not have, or is malformed
gb-211-what = The deployment's goat_host_firewall_override names a rule that is not in the baseline, names a fixed rule (loopback, established, invalid), uses a key other than drop, sources, ports, interface and reason, asks a fixed-expression rule for more than drop, or gives a reason holding a line break or another control character (the reason is written into the ruleset file). The whole override was refused and nothing was applied.
gb-211-cause-1 = a misspelled rule name, or a peering rule named for an interface this host does not carry
gb-211-cause-2 = an attempt to add a new allow rule; only named baseline rules can be changed, and other-tcp / other-udp are the named rules for a service the host also runs
gb-212-title = the host firewall is loosened without a recorded reason
gb-212-what = An override entry widens a baseline rule (more sources, other ports, another interface), or a role input is wider than the profile (goat_host_firewall_mesh_udp_ports not empty, a mesh DNS port other than 5353, a management source broader than goat_host_firewall_management_prefix_floor, or that floor set broader than /24 or /64), and no reason is recorded for that rule. Tightening is free; loosening a named rule needs a reason recorded in the deployment's configuration. Nothing was applied.
gb-212-cause-1 = the entry has no reason: key, or a blank one
gb-212-cause-2 = an entry meant to narrow widens instead (an empty sources list means any source)
gb-213-title = the host firewall would cut the session managing the host
gb-213-what = The rendered ruleset admits no SSH from the source of the session running the play (read from SSH_CONNECTION on the host), so loading it would lock the deployment out. Nothing was applied.
gb-213-cause-1 = goat_host_firewall_management_sources does not hold the address this play connects from
gb-213-cause-2 = the override drops ssh-management, narrows its sources past this address, or binds it to another interface
gb-213-cause-3 = SSH listens on another port than goat_host_firewall_ssh_port
gb-213-cause-4 = the session's source could not be read, so the check could not run
gb-214-title = the host firewall ruleset was not loaded or not confirmed
gb-214-what = Loading the rendered ruleset failed, or a fresh connection to the host did not succeed after it was loaded. A ruleset that was loaded is undone by the rollback timer the role armed before loading it.
gb-214-cause-1 = the kernel refused the ruleset (nft -c failed), for example an nftables too old for a rule
gb-214-cause-2 = the new ruleset blocks the connection the controller makes, through a path the lockout check could not see (a jump host, a NAT between controller and host)
gb-214-cause-3 = systemd-run is not available on the host, so no rollback could be armed and nothing was loaded
gb-215-title = the host firewall would close a port this host listens on
gb-215-what = Before loading, the host firewall baseline reads the sockets the host listens on (ss) and found one, on a non-loopback address, that no rule admits and that the deployment has not named as closed on purpose. Loading would cut that service without anyone deciding it. Nothing was applied.
gb-215-cause-1 = the host runs another service beside the peering (a management server, a portal, an exporter)
gb-215-cause-2 = a port the deployment means to close is not listed in goat_host_firewall_closed_listeners
gb-180-title = another run of this peering is in progress
gb-180-what = A real run of `goatbuilder federate` holds <work>/.lock from start to end, so two runs of one peering never interleave phases or overwrite each other's run journal. This run found the lock held and changed nothing.
gb-180-cause-1 = another terminal or a scheduled job is running `goatbuilder federate` for the same peering
gb-180-cause-2 = two peerings were given the same working folder (--work or FED_WORK)
gb-181-title = a resource this peering needs is held by another peering on the same host
gb-181-what = Before a peering takes a resource on a shared host (a tunnel interface, a port, a link subnet, a certificate folder), goatbuilder reads every other peering's run journal in the same work root under <root>/.allocation.lock. One of them holds the same resource on the same host, or a journal there could not be read, so nothing was claimed and nothing changed.
gb-181-cause-1 = two peerings were given the same per-peering value (a pinned value in `peerings:` or a hand-written group_vars file)
gb-181-cause-2 = a journal in the work root is damaged, so the check cannot tell what it holds
gb-182-title = the run journal cannot be read
gb-182-what = <work>/state.json is the only account of what ran for this peering, so a journal that does not parse, was written by a newer goatbuilder, or names another peering is refused rather than overwritten.
gb-182-cause-1 = the file was edited by hand or cut short
gb-182-cause-2 = a newer goatbuilder wrote it (its schema is newer than this one reads)
gb-182-cause-3 = two peerings share one working folder
gb-183-title = a federate phase failed
gb-183-what = The word the run journal records for a phase that exited non-zero, with its exit code. The run stopped there; no later phase ran.
gb-183-cause-1 = the phase's own error, printed above it (a `goat federate` exit 20 to 24, a play's failed task, a refused gate)
gb-184-title = the working folder or its lock cannot be made, taken or written
gb-184-what = A real run of `goatbuilder federate` keeps its lock and its run journal in the peering's working folder, and the allocation lock in the folder above it. One of them could not be created, locked or written, so the run did not start (or, mid-run, its journal is behind; the run says so in a failed `journal` row).
gb-184-cause-1 = the working folder is not writable by this user (the default /var/lib/goat-federation needs root or a folder made for you)
gb-184-cause-2 = the disk is full or the folder is on a read-only mount
gb-130-title = a federation input is missing
gb-130-what = federate phase 1 (preflight) found inputs the later phases need that nothing declared and nothing could derive. Every missing input is listed with where to declare it. Nothing was measured, signed or deployed.
gb-130-cause-1 = the deployment does not declare it (the edge group's group_vars, the peering's entry in goatbuilder.yaml peerings:, the inventory)
gb-130-cause-2 = the script avenue (peering-standup.sh) was run without exporting it; goatbuilder derives what it can, the script does not
gb-131-title = a port this peering needs is already held on the edge
gb-131-what = the tunnel listen port (UDP) or the edge router listen port (TCP) is held on the edge host by something other than this peering's own interface or container. NetBird holds UDP 51820 on a mesh host, which is also the WireGuard default.
gb-131-cause-1 = the tunnel listen port is the WireGuard default 51820 and NetBird's interface holds it
gb-131-cause-2 = another peering's edge router or interface already uses the port on a shared edge host
gb-132-title = the republisher metrics port is already held on the core
gb-132-what = the outbound republisher's loopback metrics port is held on the core host by something other than this peering's republisher. One port per peering on a shared core host.
gb-132-cause-1 = another peering's republisher uses the same metrics port on the core host
gb-132-cause-2 = another service listens on that loopback port
gb-133-title = the edge or core host could not be probed for its ports
gb-133-what = the read-only probe (ops/ansible/peering-preflight.yml) returned no answer for a host, or `ss` could not list its sockets, so the ports were not checked. Not a pass.
gb-133-cause-1 = the host is unreachable with the inventory the peering plays use (mesh down, wrong ansible_host, no SSH key)
gb-133-cause-2 = iproute2 (`ss`) is not installed on the host
gb-134-title = the committed slot list differs from the slot registry
gb-134-what = ops/scripts/sync-slot-anchors.py --check found live slots with no declared store or declared stores for slots that are not live. A core router restarted on this list drops or misses receive stores. Slots minted between the fix and the restart drift it again, so the restart step runs this check once more.
gb-134-cause-1 = slots were minted or revoked since the list was last regenerated and committed
gb-134-cause-2 = the list was regenerated, and a slot was minted before the core router restarted
gb-135-title = the slot registry could not be read
gb-135-what = sync-slot-anchors.py exited 2: the registry was unreadable, or it reported zero live slots. That is not a verdict on drift, and the check is not passed.
gb-135-cause-1 = FED_SLOT_DB names a file that is not the portal registry, or it cannot be opened read-only
gb-135-cause-2 = the management host named by FED_SLOT_PG_SSH is unreachable, or its portal database container differs
gb-136-title = the core has no receive store matching what the edge grants
gb-136-what = the core router's storage_manager has no store whose key expression equals an ingress scope the edge admits (from/<partner>/** by the spec's rule, or the rendered goat_zenoh_edge_ingress_key_exprs). A store wider than the grant, such as from/**, declares a subscriber the edge refuses, so data would cross and not be kept.
gb-136-cause-1 = the core keeps only a historical from/** store
gb-136-cause-2 = the core's per-partner store names a different partner or lane than the edge grants
gb-137-title = the core router's configuration could not be read
gb-137-what = preflight found the core router but could not read its configuration, so the receive stores were not checked. Reported SKIP, never PASS; FED_PREFLIGHT_STRICT=1 refuses on it.
gb-137-cause-1 = the core router reads its configuration from another path than /etc/zenoh/config.json5
gb-137-cause-2 = the account behind FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST may list containers but not exec in them
gb-138-title = no core router container was found
gb-138-what = the selection phase 3 uses (goat federate init) found no running core router on the daemon preflight read: FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST, else the local runtime. Discovery accepts the org.goat.component=zenoh-core label, *-zenoh-router names and legacy goat-zenoh.
gb-138-cause-1 = the core router runs on another host and FED_ROUTER_DOCKER_HOST does not point at it
gb-138-cause-2 = the core router is stopped, or runs under a custom name without the role label
gb-139-title = several core router containers were found
gb-139-what = more than one running container qualifies as the core router, and preflight, like phase 3, will not choose among them.
gb-139-cause-1 = an old and a new core router run side by side
gb-139-cause-2 = two stacks on one host both carry a *-zenoh-router name
gb-140-title = our CA certificate is missing or is not a CA
gb-140-what = the certificate phase 8 signs the peering leaves with does not exist, is not a PEM certificate, or is not a CA (basicConstraints CA:TRUE).
gb-140-cause-1 = FED_CA_CERT, goat_federation_ca_cert or the derived ops/ansible/enrollment/ca/<site>/root-cert.pem names a missing file
gb-140-cause-2 = a leaf certificate was named instead of the CA
gb-141-title = our CA signing key cannot be resolved
gb-141-what = the CA private key phase 8 needs is missing, does not decrypt with the age identity, is not a private key, or does not match the CA certificate. The deployment keeps it at <deploy.state_dir>/<site>-ca/ca.key.age; preflight decrypts it in memory to compare and writes no plaintext.
gb-141-cause-1 = the ca.key.age file is not on this machine
gb-141-cause-2 = the age identity (~/.config/age/keys.txt, or FED_AGE_IDENTITY) is not the one the key was encrypted to
gb-141-cause-3 = the key belongs to another CA than the certificate
gb-142-title = our federation signing key cannot be read
gb-142-what = FED_SIGNING_KEY names a file that does not exist, or whose content is neither 64 hex characters (the RFC 8032 seed) nor 44 base64.
gb-142-cause-1 = goat_federation_signing_key or FED_SIGNING_KEY names the wrong path
gb-142-cause-2 = the file holds a PEM or a public key rather than the seed
gb-143-title = the peering inventory does not reach the edge or core
gb-143-what = inventory/peering-edges.ini is missing, has no group for this peering's edge or core, lists no host in it, or does not list the group under [peering_edges:children] / [peering_cores:children]. A play limited to such a group runs against nothing and reports success.
gb-143-cause-1 = the edge or core group was never added to the deployment's inventory
gb-143-cause-2 = the committed inventory ships the host commented out
gb-144-title = the federation declaration or the profile is missing or wrong
gb-144-what = the goat context has no federation.toml, it has no valid sovereign_id or it declares a measured capability, or the profile certificate or CA bundle is missing.
gb-144-cause-1 = `goat profile init` was not run for this context, or ran as another user (sudo -E keeps HOME)
gb-144-cause-2 = federation.toml was not written, or declares transports, fabric_version, fabric_fips_image or pqc_capable
gb-145-title = the core certificate would not name every address the edge dials
gb-145-what = FED_CORE_SAN does not cover an address the edge's deployment.yml dials. A core certificate without that SAN fails name verification, and nothing is logged at INFO when it does.
gb-145-cause-1 = an endpoint was added to group_vars/<edge group>/deployment.yml after FED_CORE_SAN was set by hand
gb-146-title = docker compose is not available for phase 12
gb-146-what = the edge role deploys with `docker compose`, and this machine has no docker, no compose plugin, or a compose that reports no version.
gb-146-cause-1 = docker-compose-plugin is not installed
gb-147-title = a peering directory value is not usable
gb-147-what = FED_DIRECTORY_URL is not https, or it, FED_PACKAGE_URL, FED_COUNTERPARTY_ID or FED_CA_CERT holds characters outside a plain URL. Those values reach a command line, so the run refuses them.
gb-147-cause-1 = an http:// directory address, or a value with spaces or quotes
gb-148-title = the federation working directory is not writable
gb-148-what = the run's working directory (--work, FED_WORK, else /var/lib/goat-federation/<partner>) cannot be created or written.
gb-148-cause-1 = the default under /var/lib needs root
gb-149-title = the peering is not declared
gb-149-what = the partner is not in goatbuilder.yaml peerings:. A peering nobody declared is one nobody reviewed (contract C1).
gb-149-cause-1 = the peering was never added to the deployment's goatbuilder.yaml
gb-230-title = allocate would move a peering that already runs
gb-230-what = A peering the allocation record does not hold already runs from hand-written configuration (its edge group has hand group_vars, or the hand inventory names it), and the values allocation chose differ from the ones it runs on (its hand group_vars, else the role defaults). Rendering them would move its tunnel or ports on the next phase 2. Nothing was written.
gb-230-cause-1 = goatbuilder federate allocate run for the first time in a deployment with a peering set up by hand
gb-231-title = the hand-written peering inventory puts one name in two peering groups
gb-231-what = No peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini is hand-written, and it puts one inventory name in two peering groups. Ansible merges the group_vars of every group a name is in, so each peering would read the other's rendered values. Nothing was written.
gb-231-cause-1 = one machine listed under the same name for two peerings
gb-234-title = the site inventory does not name one edge and one core for a peering without hosts
gb-234-what = A peering declares no hosts:, so goatbuilder federate allocate takes its machines from the site inventory (the environment's inventory:, else ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini but the peering-*.ini files): the group of the peering's edge_class and of the site's other class, each named as the class, <site>_<class>, <environment>_<class> or <environment>_<site>_<class>. More than one file names them, a class has no machine or several, or the two machines are reached as different users. The message names the candidates and where it looked. Nothing was written.
gb-234-cause-1 = two inventory files define the site's class groups
gb-234-cause-2 = a class group lists several machines, or the edge class group lists none
gb-234-cause-3 = the edge and the core have different ansible_user values
gb-235-title = a named allocate would change another peering's inventory lines
gb-235-what = Every peering declares hosts:, so ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini is the whole generated peering inventory. `goatbuilder federate allocate <peering>` renders the named peering's groups into it and leaves every other line as it was; this run would change or drop a section of another peering (its hosts were changed in goatbuilder.yaml since the last allocate, or it is declared but not yet recorded). Nothing was written.
gb-235-cause-1 = another peering's hosts: changed in goatbuilder.yaml since the inventory was last rendered
gb-235-cause-2 = a peering in the generated inventory is declared but has no entry in the allocation record
gb-236-title = our core router's goat-zenoh role declarations do not resolve for admission
gb-236-what = Phase 10's admission names no route of its own (FED_CORE_PLAY and FED_CORE_RESTART are unset), and an inventory group of the site inventory (ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini, not peering-*.ini) has group_vars that configure the goat-zenoh role, so admission restarts the core through the role's own play on that group. It could not resolve that play: several groups configure the role and the peering core's address does not pick one, nothing gives the group's site_id, or no single fleet pin whose image variable the group sets names a deploy play that exists. The message names what was looked for and where. Nothing on the core changed.
gb-236-cause-1 = two inventory groups both configure the goat-zenoh role
gb-236-cause-2 = the core router group's group_vars declare no site_id and the group is not named <site>_hosts
gb-236-cause-3 = no component in ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml has the group's image variable as its var with a deploy play
gb-237-title = admission cannot write the partner into the role core's zenoh_sovereign_peerings
gb-237-what = On a goat-zenoh role core, phase 10's admission writes the partner's zenoh_sovereign_peerings entry (sovereign, and the CN of the agreement's access-control fragment subject) into ops/ansible/group_vars/<core router group>/peerings.yml, the role's own declaration, beside the fragment. It could not: the fragment names no subject CN or several, another of the group's group_vars files already sets zenoh_sovereign_peerings (two owners), an entry's sovereign is a template (it contains {"{"}{"{"}, {"{"}% or {"{"}#, so no literal id matches it and the partner would be added a second time), or peerings.yml is not YAML. Nothing on the core changed.
gb-237-cause-1 = zenoh_sovereign_peerings is set by hand in another file of the core router group's group_vars
gb-237-cause-2 = an entry of zenoh_sovereign_peerings writes its sovereign as a Jinja template, or is not a mapping with a literal sovereign
gb-237-cause-3 = zenoh_sovereign_peerings itself is a template, a mapping or a scalar, not a literal list: read as empty, it would be written back holding this partner alone
gb-237-cause-4 = the fragment phase 9 rendered names no subject, or several
gb-185-title = the partner named to `federate status` is not a declared peering
gb-185-what = `goatbuilder federate status <partner>` reads a peering declared in goatbuilder.yaml `peerings:` (on `--env`, when given). The name given is not one of them, so nothing was read.
gb-185-cause-1 = the name is misspelled, or the peering is declared under another environment's config
gb-185-cause-2 = the peering has not been declared yet
gb-186-title = the plan was not approved
gb-186-what = `goatbuilder federate deploy` executes a plan only when approval names that plan's hash: `--approve-plan <hash>`, or a person at a terminal typing yes after reading the plan and its hash. `--yes` never approves a plan. No approval was given, or it named another hash, so nothing changed.
gb-186-cause-1 = there is no terminal and --approve-plan was not passed
gb-186-cause-2 = --approve-plan names the hash of an older plan (the plan was made again since)
gb-186-cause-3 = the person answered anything but yes
gb-187-title = the plan is not the one approved
gb-187-what = The plan deploy was given is for another peering, or its hash changed between approval and execution (it is read again under the peering's lock, just before anything changes). Nothing changed. A plan that does not match its own hash is GB-201.
gb-187-cause-1 = no plan has been made yet (<work>/plan.json is missing)
gb-187-cause-2 = the plan was made again, or edited, after it was approved
gb-187-cause-3 = --plan names another peering's plan
gb-188-title = the plan no longer holds
gb-188-what = Immediately before anything changes, deploy runs the planner's live recheck: every live prerequisite is checked again and every input section is compared with a plan built now. Something did not hold (a section changed, a check was not PASS or NOT APPLICABLE or was not answered, or the plan was not READY), so the approved plan no longer describes the change, and a saved plan is not proof the environment stayed the same. Nothing changed.
gb-188-cause-1 = the agreement was composed again (a new package from either side)
gb-188-cause-2 = apply rendered the files again, or one was edited by hand
gb-189-title = a stage of `goatbuilder federate` was asked for the wrong way
gb-189-what = The stages `status`, `deploy` and `test` take the partner after them and run their own phases; `--plan` and `--approve-plan` belong to `deploy`, whose start is phase 10 or, once the admission is done by hand, 11. Nothing ran.
gb-189-cause-1 = the partner was left out, or given after a peering name instead of a stage word
gb-189-cause-2 = --to, --resume or a --from other than 10 or 11 was given to a stage
gb-189-cause-3 = --plan or --approve-plan was given outside `deploy`
gb-190-title = the admission on our core is not recorded as done for what is rendered now
gb-190-what = `deploy --from 11` skips phase 10, the by-hand admission of the partner's edge on our core, so it runs only when the journal records phase 10 as done for exactly the files rendered now. It does not, so nothing changed: either the admission was never declared, or the rendered files changed after it.
gb-190-cause-1 = deploy has not stopped at phase 10 yet, so the admission was never done for these files
gb-190-cause-2 = the files were rendered again after the admission was declared
gb-191-title = a value for confirmation 1 does not match
gb-191-what = At phase 5 or 6 a value given by flag or typed is not the one the files hold: their CA fingerprint is not their package's, their CA certificate does not carry that fingerprint, their verifying key is not 32 bytes of base64 or does not verify their half, a sovereign id is not a plain id or is not the declared counterparty, or the agreement digest differs. The run stopped; at phase 5 no keyring was written, and at phase 6 the composed agreement is left for inspection.
gb-191-cause-1 = a value was misheard or mistyped on the call
gb-191-cause-2 = the partner published a new package or CA after reading theirs aloud
gb-191-cause-3 = the two sides composed different agreements (their halves differ)
gb-192-title = confirmation 1 is waiting
gb-192-what = Phase 5 or 6 was asked to confirm (by --confirm or a value) and something is not there yet: their half or CA certificate has not arrived, a value read aloud was not given and there is no terminal to ask at, or the person did not type yes. The run stopped there, as a by-hand stop does (exit 0); a stop at phase 5 writes no keyring.
gb-192-cause-1 = the partner has not published yet
gb-192-cause-2 = one of --partner-key, --partner-ca-fingerprint, --agreement-digest is missing
gb-193-title = our verifying key cannot be read, or does not verify our half
gb-193-what = Phase 5 shows our verifying key to read aloud, derived from the Ed25519 seed that signs our package (FED_SIGNING_KEY: 64 hex characters or 32 bytes base64). It is unset, unreadable or not a seed, or the key it gives does not verify the signature on our half, so the partner would bind a key that cannot verify our package.
gb-193-cause-1 = FED_SIGNING_KEY is not set in this environment
gb-193-cause-2 = it names a file that is not the package-signing seed, or a different seed than phase 3 signed with
gb-194-title = the edge cannot be admitted on our core
gb-194-what = Phase 10 by the command (confirmation 2) did not change our core: the agreement digest has not been confirmed with the partner for the agreement in place, --approve-admission names another digest (something changed since it was read), the deployment does not say where our core's access control lives or how it restarts, their CA does not match the agreement, or the fragment could not be merged, assembled or placed (what was placed is put back).
gb-194-cause-1 = phase 6 ran without --agreement-digest (or --confirm), so the digest was never compared
gb-194-cause-2 = the files were rendered again after the summary was read
gb-194-cause-3 = FED_CORE_ACL_ORG, FED_CORE_PLAY or FED_CORE_RESTART, or FED_SLOT_* is not set
gb-194-cause-4 = their CA file holds more than the one certificate the agreement names
gb-195-title = the core's dry run or the slot check refused the admission
gb-195-what = After placing the new access control (nothing loads it before a restart), the core router's dry run failed, or the committed slot list differs from the registry or the registry could not be read. Restarting now could drop or add stores, so the files placed were put back and the core was not restarted.
gb-195-cause-1 = the assembled configuration does not pass the core's play in check mode
gb-195-cause-2 = the slot list in the repository is behind the registry
gb-196-title = the core restart failed after the admission was placed
gb-196-what = The new access control is in place on our core, and the restart through the deployment's play or command failed, so it may not be loaded.
gb-196-cause-1 = the play or restart command failed (its output is above)
gb-197-title = the admission is waiting for approval
gb-197-what = Phase 10 was asked to admit the edge (by --confirm, --approve-admission or --review-in-pr) and stopped without changing our core: the summary was written for review in a pull request, the person did not type yes, or there is no terminal and no --approve-admission. Exit 0, as a by-hand stop.
gb-197-cause-1 = --review-in-pr, or pr at the terminal
gb-197-cause-2 = no approval was given
gb-200-title = the federation plan's inputs changed
gb-200-what = an input the saved plan rests on (versions, the agreement, a rendered file, a certificate, a resource or the acceptance policy) differs from what the plan recorded, so the approval given to that plan does not cover this change.
gb-200-cause-1 = a phase rendered, minted or composed again after the plan was made
gb-200-cause-2 = the checkout, the goat binary or a declared value changed after the plan was made
gb-201-title = the federation plan is missing or altered
gb-201-what = <work>/plan.json is absent, is not a goat-federation-plan/v1 plan, or does not match its own hash, so it cannot stand for what was approved.
gb-201-cause-1 = no plan was made for this working directory
gb-201-cause-2 = the plan file was edited by hand after it was written
gb-202-title = a live prerequisite of the federation plan no longer holds
gb-202-what = rerun immediately before a change, a live phase-1 check (ports, slot list, core router, receive stores) did not pass, so nothing may change. SKIP is not a pass here.
gb-202-cause-1 = another service took a port the plan expected free
gb-202-cause-2 = a slot was minted after the slot list was fixed
gb-202-cause-3 = the core router was replaced or stopped
gb-232-title = a rendered deployment half needs a site value no declaration yields
gb-232-what = The peering declares deployment: rendered, so goatbuilder federate allocate renders the edge's and the core's deployment halves (peering-deployment.yml). Each required site value is taken from the group_vars of the environment's groups (environments[].groups) or all when one sets it, and is otherwise derived from the declaration that owns it: the core's address from peerings[].hosts.core or its line in the peering inventory, the core router's port from goat_zenoh_listen_port (group_vars, else the goat-zenoh role default), the images from the role defaults and ops/pins/fleet-pins.yaml, the core's certificate name from its name in the site inventory (ops/ansible/inventory/*.ini). One of them could be found in none of these places; the message names each place it read. Nothing is invented and nothing was written.
gb-232-cause-1 = the peering declares no hosts: and the peering inventory has no single host for its core group
gb-232-cause-2 = no role default and no fleet-pins component pins the image the message names
gb-232-cause-3 = the site values live in a group the environment does not name in environments[].groups
gb-233-title = a peering's federation.toml cannot be generated from the environment's
gb-233-what = The peering declares federation_toml: rendered, so goatbuilder federate allocate generates its federation.toml from the environment's own ops/federation/<environment>/federation.toml, the one owner of sovereign_id, term_days, cut_contact, the release sets, the accept limits and the measured path MTU, and fills [transport] from the allocated values. That file is missing, is not TOML, or lacks a required top-level value. Nothing was written.
gb-233-cause-1 = the environment has no ops/federation/<environment>/federation.toml yet
gb-233-cause-2 = sovereign_id, term_days or cut_contact is missing from it
gb-160-title = two peerings on one forwarder would share a value
gb-160-what = The peerings one forwarder relays must share no carrier interface, public port, carrier port or carrier address on it: the forwarder sends each packet by its port alone. Allocation keeps them apart across every environment the forwarder relays (by its endpoint); this refusal is the backstop for values that collide anyway.
gb-160-cause-1 = a hand edit of peering-values.yaml gives two relayed peerings one forwarder value
gb-160-cause-2 = a pin under values: (public_port, carrier_port, carrier_interface, carrier_subnet) repeats another peering's value
gb-161-title = a peering's partner id cannot name a forwarder entry
gb-161-what = The forwarder names each peering it relays by the partner id, and its role accepts 2-16 characters of a-z 0-9 and hyphen. This partner id is outside that, so the forwarder would refuse the entry.
gb-161-cause-1 = the peering's with: holds an underscore, or is one character or longer than 16
gb-162-title = one forwarder group names two forwarders
gb-162-what = The forwarder's peerings are rendered into its inventory group's group_vars, so every host in the group would relay every peering. Two peerings name the same group with different forwarder endpoints.
gb-162-cause-1 = two forwarders declared without forwarder: group:, so both took the default peering_forwarders
gb-163-title = a forwarded peering has no allocated values yet
gb-163-what = The peering declares forwarder:, so phase 2 runs the forwarder play and copies the carrier keys, and both read the group_vars goatbuilder federate allocate renders from the allocation record. The record does not hold this peering, so those files do not exist. Nothing ran.
gb-163-cause-1 = forwarder: was added to the peering and goatbuilder federate allocate was not run since
gb-203-title = two peerings would bind the same value on one machine
gb-203-what = this peering and another declared peering would bind the same WireGuard interface, tunnel UDP port or republisher metrics port, or overlapping link subnets, on the same machine (by inventory address), so one would break the other.
gb-203-cause-1 = two environments, or two hand-named inventory groups, place their edges or cores on one machine
gb-203-cause-2 = a value was pinned or declared by hand without looking at the other peerings on that machine
gb-220-title = federate backout was not given a partner it can act on
gb-220-what = goatbuilder federate backout takes one partner: a sovereign id (2-16 characters of a-z0-9-) that is not a federate subcommand. The working directory and edge group must be plain paths and names, because they go into the play's command line. Nothing ran.
gb-220-cause-1 = the partner was left out, or is not a sovereign id
gb-220-cause-2 = --work or --edge-group holds a quote, a space or a shell character
gb-221-title = the back-out found a resource it cannot show is this peering's alone
gb-221-what = The read-only survey found a resource under this peering's names that another peering holds (a WireGuard interface whose state file or peer names another partner, a carrier another peering's state names), or a core configuration it cannot prove changes by this peering alone (the live file is not seed + org fragment, partner ids overlap, a policy mixes partners). Nothing was changed on any host.
gb-221-cause-1 = two peerings were given the same interface, carrier or stack name in their group values
gb-221-cause-2 = the core's live configuration was edited by hand after the last assemble
gb-221-cause-3 = the core keeps no seed/org split (slot-pool mode), so the admission cannot be located
gb-222-title = the back-out survey did not complete
gb-222-what = ops/ansible/peering-backout.yml failed before every host of the peering answered, so there is no plan to show and nothing was changed.
gb-222-cause-1 = a host of <partner>_edge, <partner>_core or peering_forwarders is unreachable
gb-222-cause-2 = the inventory ops/ansible/inventory/peering-edges.ini does not exist in this checkout
gb-223-title = the slot recheck did not allow the core router restart
gb-223-what = Immediately before the core router restarts without this peering's admission, the committed slot list is checked against the registry, as the admission path does. It drifted, the registry could not be read, or no registry was named, so the core was not restarted. The edge side of the peering is already removed and the partner has no path to the core.
gb-223-cause-1 = slots were minted or revoked since the list was committed
gb-223-cause-2 = FED_SLOT_DB (or FED_SLOT_REGISTRY_STORE=postgres with FED_SLOT_PG_SSH) is not set for a deployment with lazy slot storage
gb-224-title = the core router was not restarted on the reviewed candidate
gb-224-what = The core step installs only the candidate configuration whose digest the survey printed. The core's files changed since the survey (so the candidate's digest differs), more than one core router was found, or the restart failed. The org fragment kept beside it as <fragment>.before-backout-<partner> is the state before the step.
gb-224-cause-1 = another change reached the core between the survey and the restart
gb-224-cause-2 = several containers qualify as the core router
gb-225-title = resources of the peering are still present after the back-out
gb-225-what = The survey run after the removal still finds resources of this peering, or the removal play stopped partway. Every step is idempotent: running the back-out again removes what is left and reports the rest as absent.
gb-225-cause-1 = a host became unreachable during the removal
gb-225-cause-2 = a unit or container would not stop
gb-226-title = our package for the counterparty could not be withdrawn from the directory
gb-226-what = The back-out asks the peering directory to delete /v1/parties/<us>/packages/<partner>, signed by our bound key. The directory refused or could not be reached. This is a warning: a listing grants nothing, and the peering's hosts are already clean. Our single entry and every other party's entry are never touched.
gb-226-cause-1 = FED_SIGNING_KEY is not set, or is not the key the directory binds to us
gb-226-cause-2 = the directory is unreachable
gb-227-title = the core still admits the partner through its own configuration
gb-227-what = The core router was configured by roles/goat-zenoh from zenoh_sovereign_peerings, which declares the partner's subject and grants as sovereign-<partner> ids. That is the deployment's configuration, not a spliced fragment, so the back-out reports it and does not edit it. The partner has no edge left to reach the core through.
gb-227-cause-1 = the core admits the peering from zenoh_sovereign_peerings rather than from the spliced fragment
x-ansible-001-title = a host was unreachable
x-ansible-001-what = ansible could not open a connection to the host over the transport it was given (ssh over the mesh, or the door).
x-ansible-001-cause-1 = this laptop is not on the goatnet's mesh profile and no door was opened
x-ansible-001-cause-2 = the host is down
x-ansible-001-cause-3 = the SSH key is not accepted (see the ssh entries)
x-ansible-002-title = sudo on the host wants a password
x-ansible-002-what = a task needed privilege escalation and the host's operator user is not NOPASSWD.
x-ansible-002-cause-1 = the host was not baselined (mgmt-host-baseline.yml sets NOPASSWD sudo)
x-ansible-002-cause-2 = the wrong remote user
x-ansible-003-title = a play assertion failed on the host
x-ansible-003-what = a task asserted a precondition, usually that a templated secret is non-empty, and it was false on the host.
x-ansible-003-cause-1 = a secret the play templates was empty in the deployer's environment (see GB-007)
x-ansible-003-cause-2 = a group_vars fact missing for this goatnet
x-ansible-004-title = a task failed on a host
x-ansible-004-what = ansible marks the task fatal; its msg field says what the module could not do.
x-ansible-004-cause-1 = see the msg in the fatal line
x-ansible-005-title = the preflight refused a stale or unsafe checkout
x-ansible-005-what = ops/scripts/ansible-preflight.sh refuses to run a play from a tree that differs from origin/main in a safety-critical path, or from a stale trunk; ansible reads role defaults from the working tree, so a stale tree templates stale pins.
x-ansible-005-cause-1 = local main behind origin
x-ansible-005-cause-2 = a feature branch that touches group_vars or roles
x-ssh-001-title = the host refused the key
x-ssh-001-what = the connection reached the host and SSH rejected every key offered.
x-ssh-001-cause-1 = your public key is not on the host's operator user (ssh-key-self-service)
x-ssh-001-cause-2 = the wrong user (hosts take the deploy.ssh_user account)
x-ssh-001-cause-3 = the key is not loaded in the agent
x-ssh-002-title = no route to the host
x-ssh-002-what = the connection never reached the host: timed out, no route, or refused on port 22.
x-ssh-002-cause-1 = the address is on a network this laptop is not on (a mesh IP without the mesh, a Tailscale IP without the tailnet)
x-ssh-002-cause-2 = the host is down
x-ssh-003-title = the host key changed
x-ssh-003-what = the host presented a different key than ~/.ssh/known_hosts holds for that address.
x-ssh-003-cause-1 = the host was rebuilt (expected after a re-image)
x-ssh-003-cause-2 = an address was reused by another machine
x-op-001-title = 1Password CLI is not signed in
x-op-001-what = op needs a session for the account before it can resolve references.
x-op-001-cause-1 = no `op signin` in this shell
x-op-001-cause-2 = OP_ACCOUNT names a different account than the one signed in
x-op-002-title = an op:// reference is malformed
x-op-002-what = op run validates every op:// value in the environment and refuses the whole run when one has fewer than three parts (vault/item/field). The variable is often one from your shell profile, not the refs file.
x-op-002-cause-1 = a variable in your shell holds a two-part reference (op://Vault/Item)
x-op-003-title = a reference does not resolve to an item
x-op-003-what = the vault, item or field named in an op:// reference does not exist, or this account cannot see it.
x-op-003-cause-1 = the item was renamed or moved to another vault
x-op-003-cause-2 = the account lacks access to the vault
x-aws-001-title = AWS rejected the credentials
x-aws-001-what = the access key is unknown to AWS, or a session token was sent where none is accepted.
x-aws-001-cause-1 = aws-vault run without --no-session (STS tokens are rejected by this account)
x-aws-001-cause-2 = a rotated key still in the keychain
x-aws-002-title = aws-vault has no credentials for the profile
x-aws-002-what = the keychain holds no credentials under the profile name, or the keychain could not be opened (headless).
x-aws-002-cause-1 = a fresh machine
x-aws-002-cause-2 = a headless session cannot prompt the keychain
x-aws-003-title = the SSM target is not connected
x-aws-003-what = Session Manager has no live agent for the instance, so the door cannot open.
x-aws-003-cause-1 = the host is down or has no route to the SSM endpoint
x-aws-003-cause-2 = the instance id in doors.yaml is stale
x-aws-004-title = the AWS CLI fell back to an expired aws login session
x-aws-004-what = the aws-vault credential for the profile is empty or malformed, so the CLI used an expired `aws login` session instead and asked to reauthenticate.
x-aws-004-cause-1 = the aws-vault credential is empty or malformed, so the CLI fell back to an expired aws login session
x-aws-004-cause-2 = AWS_VAULT_KEYCHAIN_NAME in the environment (a direnv export) points aws-vault at the wrong keychain
x-git-001-title = not a git repository
x-git-001-what = the working directory is not inside a checkout.
x-git-001-cause-1 = run from the wrong directory
x-git-002-title = the branch cannot fast-forward
x-git-002-what = local and remote histories diverged, so a --ff-only pull refuses.
x-git-002-cause-1 = local commits on main
x-git-003-title = the remote refused access
x-git-003-what = the push or fetch was refused: the SSH key is not authorised for the repository, or the remote does not exist.
x-git-003-cause-1 = the wrong GitHub account for this remote
x-git-003-cause-2 = the key is not loaded
x-gh-001-title = GitHub returned 404
x-gh-001-what = the repository, run, release or API path does not exist, or this account cannot see it.
x-gh-001-cause-1 = a typo in the slug or id
x-gh-001-cause-2 = a private resource the account is not a member of
x-gh-002-title = the GitHub CLI is not logged in
x-gh-002-what = gh has no token for github.com in this environment.
x-gh-002-cause-1 = a fresh machine
x-gh-002-cause-2 = GH_TOKEN set to an expired token
x-gh-003-title = GitHub rate-limited or forbade the call
x-gh-003-what = HTTP 403 from the API: a rate limit, or a token without the scope.
x-gh-003-cause-1 = many unauthenticated calls in a short time
x-gh-003-cause-2 = a token missing `read:packages` or `repo`
x-cosign-001-title = the signature did not verify
x-cosign-001-what = cosign found no signature for the artifact under the expected identity, or the signature does not match.
x-cosign-001-cause-1 = the artifact was rebuilt without signing
x-cosign-001-cause-2 = the wrong public key or identity
x-docker-001-title = no permission on the Docker socket
x-docker-001-what = the user is not in the docker group on that host, so every docker call is refused.
x-docker-001-cause-1 = running docker without sudo on a host where the operator user is not in the docker group
x-docker-002-title = the image could not be pulled
x-docker-002-what = the registry answered that the manifest is unknown or the pull is unauthorised.
x-docker-002-cause-1 = a tag that was never pushed, or a digest from the other GHCR org
x-docker-002-cause-2 = the host's registry login has expired
x-docker-003-title = the Docker daemon is not running
x-docker-003-what = the socket exists but nothing answers, or does not exist at all.
x-docker-003-cause-1 = docker.service stopped on the host
x-docker-003-cause-2 = a local Docker Desktop that is not launched (never launch it for goat work)
x-netbird-001-title = no such mesh profile
x-netbird-001-what = the profile named does not exist on this laptop; profiles are per goatnet.
x-netbird-001-cause-1 = the profile was never added
x-netbird-001-cause-2 = the hyphen/underscore form differs from the profile name
x-netbird-002-title = the mesh daemon is not running
x-netbird-002-what = the netbird client could not reach its own daemon.
x-netbird-002-cause-1 = the service is stopped
x-http-001-title = the service rejected the credential (401)
x-http-001-what = the request reached the service and the token or cookie was not accepted.
x-http-001-cause-1 = an expired or wrong token
x-http-002-title = the service refused the identity (403)
x-http-002-what = the credential was accepted and the identity is not allowed to do this.
x-http-002-cause-1 = the wrong one of two credentials (participant vs operator)
x-http-002-cause-2 = no grant for this surface
x-http-003-title = the service is up but its backend is not (5xx)
x-http-003-what = the front door answered with a gateway error: Traefik or Caddy is up and the service behind it is not.
x-http-003-cause-1 = the container is restarting or down
x-http-004-title = an onboarding call went to the portal
x-http-004-what = goat sent an onboarding call (invite, approve) to the portal instead of the onboarding endpoint: the portal answers 405 Method Not Allowed, or the call names a host that is only `portal`.
x-http-004-cause-1 = the context's profile has no onboard_endpoint, so onboarding calls went to the portal
x-direnv-001-title = direnv exec was given an assignment, not a command
x-direnv-001-what = `direnv exec <dir> VAR=value cmd` looks VAR=value up on PATH as the command and fails; direnv exec takes a command.
x-direnv-001-cause-1 = an environment assignment placed where direnv exec expects the command
x-dns-001-title = the name did not resolve
x-dns-001-what = the resolver has no answer for the name; mesh names resolve only while the mesh is up.
x-dns-001-cause-1 = off the mesh (mesh names are served by mesh CoreDNS)
x-dns-001-cause-2 = a typo in the name
x-tls-001-title = the TLS handshake failed
x-tls-001-what = the certificate presented was not trusted, did not match, or the handshake ended early.
x-tls-001-cause-1 = a CA this machine does not trust (goat ca add)
x-tls-001-cause-2 = a clock far enough off that the certificate reads as invalid
x-smoke-001-title = the live rows' browser toolchain is not on this machine
x-smoke-001-what = the live acceptance rows (intake-form-browser-submit, review-gate-accepts-open-field-invite) run ops/scripts/onboard-intake-smoke.sh from the deployer's laptop, and it exits 2 before touching the goatnet when ops/goat-id-console/web/e2e has no node_modules. The release did not fail on the goatnet; it failed here.
x-smoke-001-cause-1 = a laptop that never installed the console's Playwright suite
x-py-001-title = a Python module is missing
x-py-001-what = python3 could not import a module a script needs.
x-py-001-cause-1 = a fresh machine or a venv not on PATH
x-secrets-001-title = the declared secrets store is unknown
x-secrets-001-what = GOAT_SECRET_STORE or `secrets: store:` in goatbuilder.yaml names a store goat does not have, so no command can get its secrets.
x-secrets-001-cause-1 = a typo in GOAT_SECRET_STORE or in goatbuilder.yaml
x-secrets-001-cause-2 = a custom store whose ops/secrets/<name>/run is not on this checkout
x-secrets-002-title = the env-file store has no file
x-secrets-002-what = the declared store is env-file, and its file of values does not exist on this laptop.
x-secrets-002-cause-1 = the quickstart's env file was never generated
x-secrets-002-cause-2 = a goatlite whose phase 1 has not run yet (phase 1 renders the goatnet, then creates this file)
x-secrets-002-cause-3 = GOAT_SECRET_ENV_FILE points somewhere else
x-secrets-003-title = refusing to write secret values to a file git would commit
x-secrets-003-what = `secrets init` writes plaintext values, so it refuses a path that is not gitignored.
x-secrets-003-cause-1 = --file names a path inside the repository that .gitignore does not cover
x-secrets-004-title = bao:// references need the OpenBao injector
x-secrets-004-what = the environment holds bao:// references, and ops/secrets/openbao/run (the OpenBao store's injector, ADR 1203) is not on this checkout.
x-secrets-004-cause-1 = a .refs file holds bao:// lines on a checkout that predates the OpenBao store
x-secrets-005-title = a git-crypt file is still encrypted
x-secrets-005-what = the declared store is git-crypt and its file has not been unlocked on this laptop, so its values cannot be read.
x-secrets-005-cause-1 = this clone was never unlocked
x-accept-001-title = acceptance could not run a required check, so the peering is not accepted
x-accept-001-what = ops/federation/standup/acceptance.sh (phase 14) found no failure, but a required check was BLOCKED (its input or evidence was unavailable) or SKIPPED (not exercised). Such a check proves nothing, so the verdict in <work>/evidence/acceptance/result.json is INCOMPLETE and the run exits 3, not 0.
x-accept-001-cause-1 = a surface the check reads is not configured (for example the partner's REST endpoint or the SPIRE servers)
x-accept-001-cause-2 = the agreement offers nothing in one direction, so that direction's checks had nothing to send
x-accept-001-cause-3 = a probe session the check needs was not started
x-digest-001-title = no tool could read the image's digest
x-digest-001-what = ops/scripts/image-digest tried crane, skopeo and docker in turn; none is installed, or each refused, and each tool's own error is printed beneath.
x-digest-001-cause-1 = none of crane, skopeo or docker is installed
x-digest-001-cause-2 = the registry refused this laptop's credentials (each tool's error says which)
x-digest-001-cause-3 = the tag does not exist yet (its image build has not finished)
